From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gpu31-mesa <gpu31-mesa@localhost>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] kbase: fix n_slots off-by-one in kbase_kmod_submit_job

The atom batch is: [fence-wait atom, optional] + job atom +
fence-trigger atom.  Every atom needs its own table slot
(kbase_alloc_slots_locked hands out atom_number values; the trigger
atom's pre_dep[0] points at job_slot, and cleanup iterates n_slots
entries).  The old initializer claimed one slot while always emitting
at least job + trigger (two atoms), so the unconditional
job_slot/trig_slot assignment consumed two slots[idx++] entries from
a one-entry reservation.  Without an in-fence this overran the slots
array (out-of-bounds read at slots[1], n_slots=1 cleanup leaked one
busy bit); with an in-fence the count happened to be right (1 -> 2 ->
wait+job+trigger = 2... no: 1+1=2 allocated vs 3 consumed, same
overrun).

Count job + trigger up front (n_slots = 2), plus one more when an
in-fence wait atom is emitted.

Provenance: partners G31_Devfreq/G31_Gem nudged the count
(n_slots = 2 /* job + trigger */); the expedient local patch here
/fixed/ it.  Kept as its own patch (not folded into 0001) so the
kbase backend patch stays pristine.

The write-only `bo->mk_flags = alloc.flags` noted in the same review
is intentionally NOT part of this patch: the field + store live only
in that uncommitted side tree (never in the repo's 0001 or in-tree
kbase_kmod.c), so there is nothing to remove here.
---
 src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c b/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c
index 3058e1b..e42f7c9 100644
--- a/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c
+++ b/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c
@@ -1198,6 +1198,6 @@ kbase_kmod_submit_job(struct pan_kmod_dev *dev,
                                              .stream_fd = kdev->tl_fd}};
    uint8_t slots[3];
-   unsigned n_atoms = 0, n_slots = 1;
+   unsigned n_atoms = 0, n_slots = 2; /* job + trigger */
    uint32_t wait_slot = 0, job_slot, trig_slot;
    int in_fd = -1, out_fd = -1;
    int64_t deadline;
